and a lost Turing theorem as a basis for all kinds of magic. This magic is tightly controlled and performed on machines rather then on human brains allowing a modicum of control of the consequnce. What the people who use this do is summon things or energy from beyond and force them to do their busywork - therefore Computational Demonology.<br /> <br /> This secret service recruits or kills people who dabble in the occult, and is filled with clueless idiots who got a government job because they coded a screensaver that could eat Bristol, played too much with grandfather's strange eyebending knives or inherited a strange genetic condition that made them yearn for the sea and have a strange, rubbery complexion. From this cesspool of bureacracy you end up having a couple actually competent people trying to stave of the end of the world. The only problem is that it has already started, and the stars are nigh. The only question is if the Laundry and its agents will manage to keep humanity alive until the stars are Wrong Again. Now add an American secret service that runs zombie and possesion drones and tends to be really messed up, EU meetings with other agencies that fight against the Things From Beyond, diplomatic meetings with the Deep Ones, the Russians and the Chinese who cannot decide if they want to help you or kill you and endless cults who suddenly have their spells and rituals working.<br /> <br /> This is the setting of the Laundry series. There's also an awesome RPG set in the same setting done by Cubicle 7. The RPG has the single best monster manual I have ever seen - 140 pages - 4 pages of rules, 136 pages of interviews, emails, commercials, photographs, investigations, reports, fliers, scrawled text and ebay auctions.<br /> <br /> The Laundry would be a pretty cool inspiration for a Mage game like you're positing. It has the X-Files and Supernatural vibe you're looking for, it just adds the Cthulhu Mythos and British humor to the mix.<br /> <br /> Oh and if you like the concept, you might also be interested in looking up Delta Green - a X-Files meets Supernatural meets Cthulhu RPG about a conspiracy in the american federal agencies that fights against things that go bump in the night and covers up their existence no matter the cost to their private lives or job security. It's pretty cool, but I'd be writing another wall of text about that then :P There is a kickstarter for the new edition, though.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Two years later, the campaign has progressed, stopped due to some of the players leaving the country, and I have read all the Laundry Files books. And today, the campaign is coming back to life!<br /> <br /> Thank you A LOT for the book suggestion, Asmodai! =D","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6470-sardonis","name":"Sardonis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6470_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"47"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}